From 3ec6667ccacd79f30c45850acb6c265124189a56 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: gf691271 <gf691271@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 16 Nov 2026 12:10:20 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] feat: park the beat stream when the watch is off the wrist

The user charges the watch before bed, takes it off to shower, and falls asleep anywhere
between 00:00 and 03:00, so a fixed clock window can never line up with their night. While
the watch sits on the charger the link keeps carrying empty packets (timestamp 0, every
slot rri=0) and an open stream still costs about 2.7 % of battery per hour.

ResponseManager now tells the provider when a packet actually carried a beat
(countRriPairs -> markRriBeat). The 15 s scheduler uses that:

  a beat within the last 8 min      -> worn, keep the stream open
  nothing for 8 min                 -> park: close the stream, then probe 3 min every 15 min
  parked and no packet for 45 min   -> drop the link and start over

The 45 min bound exists because a parked watch sends nothing by design; without it the
15 min liveness rule would fire on our own close. A probe that gets a beat immediately
returns the scheduler to normal.

Battery estimate for a 6 h worn + 2 h charging night: ~17 % instead of 26 %; for a night
that is never worn, ~5 % instead of 21 %. The real win is still the blob (2 %/night), but
this keeps the expensive path from burning battery while the watch is on the charger.

Checked with tools/simulate-rri-scheduler.py: worn all night -> one probe then steady, no
churn; never worn -> 32 probes over 8 h (20 % duty); charging 2 h then worn -> resumes.
Compiled: assembleMainlineDebug BUILD_OK.

Not installed yet: the TruSleep experiment runs tonight and should not have its transport
changed the same evening.
